# ---- Build artifacts ----
hub/target/
**/target/
*.o
*.obj
*.exe
*.dll
*.lib
*.exp
*.a
*.def
# MQL compiled outputs
*.ex4
*.ex5

# ---- Rust ----
# (Cargo.lock for a binary app is intentionally tracked; do not ignore)

# ---- Runtime data / logs ----
*.log
*.jsonl
# Atomic-write temps carry a pid suffix (e.g. users.json.tmp.1234,
# audit.jsonl.gz.tmp.1234) - the exact name alone does not match them.
*.tmp.*
# Audit rotation archives: audit.jsonl.1.gz .. audit.jsonl.10.gz do NOT match
# *.jsonl (they end in .gz), so they would appear untracked after a rotation.
*.jsonl.*
# Test scratch: the Python suites write these next to themselves and clean
# them up on success; a crashed run leaves them behind, never to be tracked.
tools/_hub_*
memwatch*.csv
.memwatch*
audit.jsonl
# M9 user table: holds salted password hashes, never belongs in git.
users.json
users.json.tmp
# Notification channel store: holds PLAINTEXT bot tokens / webhook secrets
# (Telegram bot_token, WeCom/DingTalk secrets). Same reason as users.json.
alert_channels.json
alert_channels.json.tmp
# CURVE (§19.1): the hub keypair holds a SECRET - never versioned, never
# shared. The allow-list is per-deployment config, not source, so it is not
# versioned either (a committed allow-list is a list somebody forgets to edit).
curve_hub.keys
curve_hub.keys.tmp
curve_clients.txt
config_revisions.jsonl
commands.jsonl

# ---- WorkBuddy project runtime data ----
.workbuddy-ai/

# ---- Old libzmq backup (kept on disk, not versioned) ----
third_party/mql-zmq-master.4.2.1.bak/

# ---- Junk: Windows system cache accidentally captured under hub/ ----
# (some build step expanded %SystemDrive% into a literal folder)
hub/%SystemDrive%/

# ---- Built libzmq prefix (regenerated by the build, not source) ----
third_party/zmq-prefix/
third_party/zmq-prefix.4.2.1.bak/

# ---- Test / tool output artifacts ----
*.out

# ---- Images (binary; not project source) ----
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.png
*.gif
*.ico
*.bmp
